.\" Man page for ax25common.conf
.\"
.TH AX25COMMON.CONF 5 "11 Aug 2026" "ax25-apps" "Linux System Administration"
.SH NAME
ax25common.conf \- shared loop port configuration of ax25netd and ax25tcpd
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ax25common.conf
configures the loop port of
.BR ax25netd (8).
Both the daemon itself and
.BR ax25tcpd (8),
which in its default setup connects to the same loop port, read this
file, so the endpoint a client connects to can never drift away from
the endpoint the daemon listens on.  Each line is one directive:
.PP
.RS
.B loop socket
.I path
.br
.B loop tcp
.I port
.br
.B loop group
.I name
.br
.B loop group
.I gid
.br
.B loop group
.B all
.RE
.PP
.B loop socket
enables the unix domain socket of the loop port at
.IR path .
Without it the loop port is only reachable over TCP.  The value
.B no
(or
.B 0
) disables the socket again.  The socket file is owned by the user the
daemon drops privileges to (or the daemon run user), so connecting
requires write access to the socket file, i.e.\& membership in the
group named by
.B loop group
.IR name .
The conventional group for AX.25 users is
.B hams
(see axspawn.conf).
.B loop group all
makes the socket accessible to every local user; a numeric
.I gid
is accepted as well.
.PP
.B loop tcp
enables the TCP listener of the loop port on
.IR port
(the default is
.BR 8100 ).
.B loop tcp yes
and
.B loop tcp no
switch the listener on and off without changing the port.
.PP
A missing file is not an error and leaves the defaults in place: unix
socket disabled, TCP listener on port 8100, socket group = the daemon
run user.
.PP
Blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored.
.SH FILES
.I @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/ax25common.conf
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ax25netd (8),
.BR agwpe.conf (5),
.BR ax25tcpd (8),
.BR ax25 (3),
.BR axports (5)
